      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that two randomly chosen members of a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10:**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is given by the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5:**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5 is given by the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability:**\n\n   The probability that both chosen members are girls is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the fraction:**\n\n   Simplify \\(\\frac{10}{45}\\) by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 5:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{10 \\div 5}{45 \\div 5} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
