      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this probability problem, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10.**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members from 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, and \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.**\n\n   Similarly, the number of ways to choose 2 girls from 5 is given by \\( \\binom{5}{2} \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both chosen members are girls.**\n\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes.\n\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{both girls}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the fraction.**\n\n   To simplify \\( \\frac{10}{45} \\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 10 and 45, which is 5.\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{10 \\div 5}{45 \\div 5} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]",
